<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhillama V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1173–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhillama V was a Seuna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> ruler who reigned from about 1173 to 1192 and made his dynasty an independent power. Exploiting the collapse of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, he seized Kalyani and the northern Chalukya provinces around 1187 and founded Devagiri (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>) as his capital. His advance south was halted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii">Veera Ballala II</a>, who defeated him in 1190.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhillama-v.jpg" alt="Bhillama V" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yadavas_of_Devagiri._Bhillama_V._1185-1193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a> inscription, Bhillama was a son of Karna and a grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> ruler Mallugi; the different genealogy given by the 13th-century court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemadri">Hemadri</a>, who flourished a century later, is thought unreliable. The Yadavas had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of Kalyani. After Mallugi, his elder son Amara-gangeya and Amara-gangeya's son Amara-mallugi ruled in quick succession, followed by Kaliya-Ballala, probably a usurper. Karna, Mallugi's younger son, was probably a subordinate officer.</p><p>In the disorder after Mallugi's death Bhillama carved out a principality of his own by capturing forts in and around the Konkan. He defeated the rulers of Srivardhana and Pratyanta-gada (modern Torna), then defeated and killed the ruler of Mangalaveshtaka (modern Mangalwedha), and at last seized power at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> capital, Sinnar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>While his nominal overlords, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Kalachuris, Bhillama turned north against Lata (southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. His Mutugi inscription of 1189 boasts that he troubled the Paramaras and the Chaulukyas, and his general Jahla is said to have won a battle by driving a mad elephant into the enemy army. The raids won no territory, and the Naddula Chahamana ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kelhana">Kelhana</a> forced him to retreat.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Ballala II drove the Chalukya Someshvara IV to shelter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>, Bhillama forced the exhausted Hoysala army back and took Kalyani. Ballala regrouped at Dvarasamudra and by June 1189 had taken Banavasi and Nolambavadi. Bhillama marched against him with 200,000 infantry and 12,000 cavalry, but was decisively defeated; his general Jaitrapala fled to Lokkigundi (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a>), where Ballala captured the fort and killed him. The Malaprabha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a>–Hoysala border for the next two decades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At the end his kingdom reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> river to the Malaprabha and took in nearly all of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, apart from the Shilahara-ruled Konkan, and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. His son Jaitugi succeeded him. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> inscription of 1198 says Ballala whetted his sword on Bhillama's head, which has led to speculation that he died in battle, but Ballala's Gadag inscriptions of 1192 boast only of killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaitrasimha">Jaitrasimha</a>, and Bhillama most probably died a natural death.</p><p>Bhillama patronised the scholar Bhaskara, teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjuna">Nagarjuna</a>, author of the Yogaratnamala. An inscription of 1189–90 records his donation to the Vitthal Temple at Pandharpur, where he is styled Chakravartin Yadava, and another his donations to the Trikuteshvara Shiva temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadag">Gadag</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhillama V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Seuna Yadava ruler <b>Bhillama V</b> took the northern provinces and Kalyani, while the Hoysala Veera Ballala II defeated the Chalukya forces in the south, and Someshvara IV disappears from the records c. 1189.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When Chalukya power collapsed, <b>Bhillama V</b> proclaimed independence around 1187, founded the fortress city of Devagiri (later Daulatabad) and fought the Hoysalas for control of the former Chalukya territories.</blockquote><a href="/state/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
